extends layout.pug

block title
    title tomokagízo

block body
    .head
        img(src="/static/atinu.png", alt="Atinú logo", width="100px")
        h1 tomokagízo no Atinú
        p buoñorok a di tomokagízo no Atinú!<br>(Welcome to the Atinú homepage!)
    .main 
        h2 About
        p
        | Welcome to the project that took me 5yrs to complete (i started in 2020).<br>
        br
        | Atinú is a conlang, more specifically an auxlang which was designed to be easy to learn and use in literature, poetry, music, etc.
        | It has its own pseudo-history, culture, writing system, and philosophy... kinda like any natural language.
        | Main roots of it are PIE, proto-germanic, latin, japanese, and various other languages :P

        h2 Pseudo-history
        p
        | It originates from a small nomadic tribe called the Atinitose (literally: Atinú humans) that lived near modern-day germany, switzerland, and italy.
        | They were mainly poets that traveled around the world to perform for food and other necessities.
        | Eventually, they travel to asia, and incorporate many of the languaes and philosophies they encounter into their own language.<br>
        br
        | People who usually left the nomadic life and settled down developed a slight accent (settler's accent) which had the sounds /t d f v/ unlike the nomads
        | Who used /t̪ d̪ ɸ β/ instead.<br>
        | So if you want to sound more sophisticated, use the nomadic accent, otherwise the settler's accent is fine.<br>
        br
        | Thats about it :P

        h2 Philosophy
        p
        | The main thing about Atinú is that it only has 190 or so <i>roots</i>!
        | However, the gender/noun-class system has 3 forms, xert (heart/abstract/root), oso (animate/alive), and śuk (inanimate/dead).
        | Changing the gender of a root along with context can change the meaning drastically, for example:<br>
        | sakan: to fish, to be like fish, peaceful, wise<br>
        | sakano: fish, marine life<br>
        | sakanuk: dead fish, foolish being (insult)<br>
        br
        | This is supposed show the cycle between life and death, and how it is a constant cycle, spinning like a wheel :P<br>
        | Yes i know... very yin-yang... dont make fun of me -w- hehehe<br>
        br
        | One more thing is that no word has a concrete meaning(?), you just know what it vaguely represents, and the way you structure the sentence.
        | The context of the conversation will be deciding factor for the actual meaning of the words.
        | Sort of the fluidity that could help with poetry, and also similar to the philosophy of "goin with the flow" lol.

